{
 "result": "csvsum.py reads a CSV from stdin and prints each cat with its val sum to 2 decimals, sorted by cat. It streams lines and accumulates into a defaultdict. I generated a 2,000,000-row d.csv with 50 categories. The assay compares the output to an independent reference computed in Python. It scores 1/runtime, which is 0 if the output is wrong. A small hand-made case gave the expected sums (a 2.00, b 4.00). One timed run of the script alone took about 0.6 s on the 2M-row file. The assay baseline was 1.62 runs/s, and the adopted variant scored 1.68, a gain of about 5%. I did not test edge cases such as empty input or quoted fields.",
 "calls": 7,
 "secs": 44.0
}
